# --- s007 Prologue q.5 (= Ord. prol. p. 5 qq. 1–2, nn. 217 ff.)
n("n174","s007-01","Romanos .31.","la","textual","A *.31.* is a misprint (no Romans 31); B (b-0016, col. 2) *Ro. xiij.*, and so Scotus, *Ord.* prol. n. 222: *Contra: Rom. 13: Finis legis est dilectio*. The wording is a conflation (Rom 13:10 *plenitudo legis est dilectio*; 1 Tim 1:5 *finis praecepti est caritas*), already in Scotus. B checked.","verified")
n("n175","s007-01","Boecius de trinitate","la","source","Boethius, *De Trinitate* c. 2 (speculative science divided into *naturalis, mathematica, theologica*); the argument is Scotus’s *Ord.* prol. n. 219, nearly verbatim (*Boethius De Trinitate assignat tres partes speculativae, quarum una est theologia*).","verified")
n("n176","s007-02","Godofredi de fontibus quodlibeto .10. q. 2.","la","source","Godfrey of Fontaines on the source of practical ‘extension’. Scotus names no one; the identification and the reference are Gorris’s. B agrees (*gotfre. de fontibus. qlibe. x. q. ij.*). Not checked against Godfrey’s *Quodlibeta* (ed. Philosophes Belges).","to-verify")
n("n177","s007-02","doctoris solemnis quodlibeto .8°. q. 1.","la","source","*Doctor solemnis* = Henry of Ghent. The view that a habit is practical from its end is Henry’s; *Quodl.* VIII q. 1 is Gorris’s reference (B: *qlibet. viij. q. i.*). Not verified in Henry’s *Quodlibeta*.","to-verify")
n("n178","s007-03","Johannis Roditori","la","source","B: *fratris Johis roditoris*. The name is unexplained. One candidate is John of Reading OFM (d. 1346), a Scotist whose prologue discusses whether theology is practical, but he is later than Scotus and cannot be the target of the *Ordinatio*. Another is John Rodington OFM (d. 1348), equally late. Gorris may be naming a later holder of the position Scotus rejects, as Scotist commentators often did. Unresolved.","conjecture")
n("n179","s007-04","articulo .9. q. 2.","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Summa quaestionum ordinariarum*, on the end of theology and the twofold act of the will. The reference is as given by A and B; it has not been checked against the Badius edition (1520). The question on the end of theology is usually cited as art. 8.","to-verify")
n("n180","s007-04","quodlibeto suo .5°. q. 4.","la","source","Gorris lists five Henrician Quodlibets for this question (V q. 4, VIII q. 1, IV q. 11, XIII q. 2, I q. 9), and VI q. 2 and XII q. 22 below (§ 8). He clearly worked from a full Henry, or from a Scotist apparatus that cited one. B agrees in all the numerals checked (b-0016/17). The individual references have not been verified.","to-verify")
n("n181","s007-05","Guillermi varronis in anglia. et Garronis in gallia","la","source","William of Ware OFM, *In I Sent.* prol. q. 4 (theology not practical because its object is not contingent; and q. 4 again in § 6 for theology as *contemplativa*). Gorris’s gloss explains the two forms of the name current in the schools, *Varro* (English) and *Garro* (French/continental). B: *garronis*. B checked.","certain")
n("n182","s007-05","Henrici natalis","la","source","Almost certainly Hervaeus Natalis OP (Hervé Nédellec, d. 1323), a Breton (*Britonis origine*), Dominican (*predicatoris ordine*), and author of the *Defensio doctrinae fratris Thomae* (hence *defensoris theologie officio*?). *Henrici* is then an error, shared by B, for *Heruei*. His *Quodlibet* I q. 3 does not fit chronologically as a target of the *Ordinatio* prologue unless it reflects Paris disputations of c. 1307. The identification is very probable; the reference has not been verified.","conjecture")
n("n183","s007-06","doctoris deuoti li. p°. q. 3.","la","source","*Doctor devotus* = Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* prooem. q. 3 (theology is *gratia ut boni fiamus*, an *habitus affectivus*). Scotus, *Ord.* prol. p. 5, quotes the ‘fourth way’ without naming him.","certain")
n("n184","s007-06","Egidii de Roma li. 1.","la","source","Giles of Rome OESA, *In I Sent.* prol. (theology as *scientia affectiva*). Gorris gives no question number. The ascription is standard in the Scotist apparatus.","certain")
n("n185","s007-07","Godofredi de fontibus quodlibeto .13. q. 1.","la","source","Godfrey of Fontaines, *Quodl.* XIII q. 1 (theology has two habits, one practical and one speculative). The reference is plausible but has not been checked.","to-verify")
n("n186","s007-07","sancti Thome prima parte summe. q. 1. articulo. 4.","la","source","Thomas Aquinas, *ST* I q. 1 a. 4: sacred doctrine is both speculative and practical, but more speculative. Scotus thought this not worth refuting, because it makes one simple habit both at once.","verified")
n("n187","s007-08","quodlibeto .12. q. 22.","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* VI q. 2 and XII q. 22, for the medicine analogy. Not checked against Henry.","to-verify")
n("n188","s007-10","praxis est actus alterius potentie quam intellectus","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 228, verbatim: *praxis … est actus alterius potentiae quam intellectus, naturaliter posterior intellectione, natus elici conformiter rationi rectae ut sit rectus*. Gorris’s ‘three particles’ follow nn. 229–233. The corollary that only an act of the will is *praxis* is n. 230.","verified")
n("n189","s007-10","electio recta eget recta ratione","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* VI.2, 1139a22–26, as used in *Ord.* prol. n. 233 (*electio recta necessario requirit rationem rectam*).","verified")
n("n190","s007-11","vtputa florenos","la","commentary","The illustration with florins (gold coins of Florence, then in general circulation) is Gorris’s own and is not in Scotus. It renders the psychology of n. 234 (the commanded act is *praxis* only *quasi per accidens*) in schoolroom terms.","certain")
n("n191","s007-11","intellectus extensione fit practicus","la","source","Aristotle, *De anima* III.10, 433a14–15, in its scholastic *auctoritas* form (*Auctoritates Aristotelis*: *intellectus speculativus extensione fit practicus*). Scotus builds the whole question on it (‘practical knowledge receives such extension’). Cf. *Ord.* prol. n. 235.","certain")
n("n192","s007-11","in prima determinatione extensione","la","textual","B: *in prima extensione*, without *determinatione*. B checked (b-0018).","verified")
n("n193","s007-11","aut intellectus appetitiuus est electio","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* VI.2, 1139b4–5 (*ὀρεκτικὸς νοῦς ἡ προαίρεσις ἢ ὄρεξις διανοητική*). A and B read *electio* as subject (‘choice is either appetitive intellect or intellective appetite’).","verified")
n("n194","s007-11","sanctus doctor","la","textual","So A and B (*sctũs doctor*, b-0018). Elsewhere Gorris’s *doctor* without qualification is Scotus. *Sanctus doctor* probably means Thomas, canonised in 1323; cf. *ST* I-II q. 13 a. 1, where choice is substantially an act of the will. It is not obviously a misprint for *subtilis*, since both witnesses agree.","uncertain")
n("n195","s007-13","duplex est finis scilicet quo et quod","la","commentary","The distinction between *finis quo* and *finis quod* (or *cuius gratia*) reconciles the Aristotelian authorities on whether practical and speculative are distinguished by end or by object. This is Gorris’s harmonisation; Scotus himself (*Ord.* prol. p. 5, the article on whence a habit is practical) argues that the end does not constitute it practical.","to-verify")
n("n196","s007-14","quinque conclusiones","la","source","The five conclusions summarise Scotus’s solution in *Ord.* prol. p. 5 qq. 1–2 on necessary and contingent theology, in created and divine intellects, with *ostensio* and *ratificatio*: necessaries are practical by the object’s conformity prior to volition, contingents only through the determining will. The paragraph numbers in the Vatican edition (c. nn. 314–332) have not been checked; the Logic Museum text available covers only nn. 217–235.","to-verify")
n("n197","s007-16","Ad hunc articulum","la","textual","B: *In hunc articulum*.","verified")
n("n198","s007-16","velut videar","la","textual","A *velut* (‘as if’) gives a weak sense. B reads *vel vt videar* (‘or so as to be seen’), which completes the contrast (for the love of God *or* for show), and the translation follows it. B checked (b-0019).","verified")
n("n199","s007-17","primi vel secundi modi","la","source","Aristotle, *APo* I.4, 73a34–b5: the first and second modes of *per se* predication. The rule cited is used in Scotus’s reply that faith is not a speculative habit.","certain")
n("n200","s007-18","per astrologiam methaphisicam","la","textual","So A and B (*ꝑ astrologiã me/taphisicam*, b-0019). Boethius’s three speculative parts are physics, mathematics and theology, so *astrologia* (a part of mathematics) makes no sense here. The intended reading must be *per theologiam metaphysicam*, i.e. Boethius’s ‘theology’ is philosophical theology, metaphysics, which is Scotus’s answer to n. 219. If the error is mechanical, B’s agreement is another error shared with A.","conjecture")
n("n201","s007-01","Romans 13","en","translation","Corrected from A’s impossible *31*, with B and Scotus; see Latin note.")
n("n202","s007-02","the Solemn Doctor","en","translation","*Doctor solemnis*, the standard title of Henry of Ghent.")
n("n203","s007-03","John “Roditor”","en","translation","Left in Gorris’s form; the identification is unresolved (see Latin note).")
n("n204","s007-05","Brother Henry Natalis","en","translation","Probably Hervaeus Natalis OP; see Latin note.")
n("n205","s007-06","the Devout Doctor","en","translation","*Doctor devotus*: Bonaventure.")
n("n206","s007-11","for instance florins","en","translation","Gorris’s own example; see Latin note.")
n("n207","s007-11","the holy Doctor","en","translation","*sanctus doctor*: probably Thomas Aquinas (see Latin note).")
n("n208","s007-14","showing and ratification","en","translation","*ostensio* and *ratificatio*: the object’s displaying of what is to be done, and the will’s confirming determination of it.")
n("n209","s007-16","or so as to be seen by the people","en","translation","Following B *vel ut videar* for A *velut videar*.")
n("n210","s007-18","by “astrology” Boethius means metaphysics","en","translation","So both prints. The sense required is surely ‘by theology Boethius means metaphysics’; see Latin note.")
# --- B collation (full): B16R–B19L
n("n1024","s007-02","ad causam finalem","la","textual","B: *ad cognitionem finalem*.","certain")
n("n1025","s007-02","incipit talem extensionem","la","textual","B: *accipit talem extensionem*, which gives the sense of Scotus's article ('from what practical knowledge receives such extension'); A's *incipit* ('begins such extension') hardly construes. The translation follows B (see note n1026).","certain")
n("n1026","s007-02","receive such extension","en","translation","Follows B *accipit*; A prints *incipit* (see note n1025).","certain")
n("n1027","s007-04","et ex alibi","la","textual","B: *et etiam alibi*.","certain")
n("n1028","s007-04","Preterea tertio cuius","la","textual","B omits *tertio*.","certain")
n("n1029","s007-04","et sumit minorem ex opinione","la","textual","B: *et sumit rationem ex* (omitting *opinione*), followed by *qlib. xiij. q. ij.*","certain")
n("n1030","s007-05","Vnde concludit theologiam","la","textual","B: *vbi concludit*.","certain")
n("n1031","s007-07","alia est doctrina discordans","la","textual","B: *Alia est opinio discordans*.","certain")
upd("n186",append="B: *Secunda est Thome* (omitting *sancti*), with the same reference.")
n("n1032","s007-07","secundam improbationem non iudicat dignam","la","textual","B: *Secundum in probatione non iudicat dignum*, an evident corruption; A gives the sense. B also omits *simul* before *sit practicus*.","certain")
n("n1033","s007-08","quodlibeto .6. q. 2. et","la","textual","B omits this reference to Henry's *Quodlibet* VI, q. 2. It reads only *idem quolibet. xij. q. xxij.*","certain")
n("n1034","s007-11","in. 6°. c. 2 in fi.","la","textual","B: *vi. ethi.* only, without the chapter reference.","certain")
